 ## tab-keys.mjs
 
-### §stable-tab-keys — closing a tab never renumbers the others
-- Since: 2026-09-02
-- Decision: a key is assigned once from `next` and is never reused or shifted. Closing a tab leaves a gap (`t1,t3`).
-- Forbidden: renumbering the remaining keys after a close to make them contiguous.
-- Reason: the key is the React key and the URL state. Shifting keys remounts every tab to the right of the closed one
-  and drops its scroll position and unsaved form input (incident 2026-08-29, reproduced by the "close keeps keys" test).
-- Evidence: `tab-keys.test.mjs`
+### §contiguous-tab-keys — closing a tab renumbers the remaining tabs
+- Since: 2026-10-22 (supersedes §stable-tab-keys, 2026-09-02)
+- Decision: `closeTab` renumbers the remaining tabs in order to `t1…tn` and resets `next` to `n + 1`, so the keys and
+  the URL state (`?tabs=`) are always contiguous. Closing `t2` of `t1,t2,t3` gives `t1,t2`; the next open is `t3`.
+- Rejected: §stable-tab-keys — assign each key once from `next` and never shift it, leaving a gap (`t1,t3`) after a
+  close. Overridden by the maintainer on 2026-10-22.
+- Reason: maintainer decision to keep the keys contiguous.
+- Known cost: the key is also the React key, so a close remounts every tab to the right of the closed one and drops
+  its scroll position and unsaved form input (the failure from incident 2026-08-29). State that must survive a close
+  needs an identity other than this key.
+- Evidence: `tab-keys.test.mjs` ("close renumbers keys", "open after close continues contiguously")
